WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:08.185
<v Rachel Evans>Morning everyone! Hope you all had a good long weekend. Anyone do anything fun?</v>

00:00:09.894 --> 00:00:16.931
<v Jessica Tran>Hey! I went hiking up at Lake Pine, got absolutely eaten alive by mosquitos but it was worth it.</v>

00:00:18.197 --> 00:00:24.123
<v Marcus Lee>Nice! I mostly just caught up on sleep and watched the new season of 'Cargo Kings.'</v>

00:00:24.972 --> 00:00:30.157
<v Samantha Ortiz>I tried that new bakery on 5th—Sugar & Steam. Highly recommend the cardamom buns.</v>

00:00:30.837 --> 00:00:37.503
<v Derek Patel>Morning all. I was on dad duty, lots of playground time. Ready to get back to adult conversations!</v>

00:00:38.009 --> 00:00:47.639
<v Rachel Evans>Alright, let’s get started. First quick update: we’re at 97% completion on the annual compliance training. Only 4 folks left, I’ll follow up with them directly.</v>

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:56.789
<v Jessica Tran>Second update: The new onboarding checklist is live in BambooHR. Please use the updated version for any new hires starting next week.</v>

00:00:58.209 --> 00:01:08.949
<v Marcus Lee>Thanks Jess. Now, main agenda: we need to finalize our AI Tools Acceptable Use Policy. IT flagged some concerns about people using ChatPilot and DraftGenie with sensitive shipment data.</v>

00:01:09.391 --> 00:01:17.909
<v Samantha Ortiz>Yeah, I’ve seen a few folks using those tools for customer emails. We need to be clear on what’s okay and what’s not.</v>

00:01:19.537 --> 00:01:28.796
<v Derek Patel>I pulled some usage stats: 18 employees used ChatPilot last month, mostly for drafting internal docs. Only 2 accessed it for anything with customer info.</v>

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:39.710
<v Rachel Evans>So, proposal: Only allow AI assistants that are on our approved vendor list—currently ChatPilot and DraftGenie—for internal, non-confidential documents. Anything with customer or shipment data needs manager approval.</v>

00:01:41.012 --> 00:01:47.308
<v Jessica Tran>Do we want to specify what counts as 'confidential'? I feel like people are confused about that.</v>

00:01:47.944 --> 00:01:53.129
<v Marcus Lee>Good point. Maybe we can add examples—like, shipment manifests, customer addresses, pricing quotes, etc.</v>

00:01:54.489 --> 00:02:01.155
<v Samantha Ortiz>And what about using AI for HR stuff? Like, can I use DraftGenie to help write performance reviews?</v>

00:02:02.946 --> 00:02:09.983
<v Derek Patel>I’d say yes, as long as you don’t include any personal identifiers or salary info. Just keep it generic.</v>

00:02:10.889 --> 00:02:17.926
<v Jessica Tran>I’m a little worried this will slow people down. We’re already stretched thin, and now we’re adding more approvals?</v>

00:02:18.858 --> 00:02:28.117
<v Rachel Evans>I get that, Jess, but we have to balance efficiency with data security. We’ve already had two near-misses with sensitive info going into external tools.</v>

00:02:28.952 --> 00:02:36.360
<v Marcus Lee>Maybe we can make the approval process lightweight? Like, a quick Slack message to your manager, not a formal ticket.</v>

00:02:36.798 --> 00:02:42.724
<v Samantha Ortiz>Agreed. And we can review the policy in 3 months to see if it’s causing bottlenecks.</v>

00:02:43.573 --> 00:02:56.165
<v Derek Patel>So, to recap: Only use ChatPilot and DraftGenie for internal, non-confidential docs. Anything with customer, shipment, or HR personal data needs manager approval—just a Slack message is fine. We’ll add examples to the policy.</v>

00:02:56.972 --> 00:03:01.787
<v Jessica Tran>Alright, I can live with that as long as we revisit it soon.</v>

00:03:02.775 --> 00:03:10.553
<v Rachel Evans>Decision made: We’re adopting the new AI Tools Acceptable Use Policy as discussed. I’ll communicate this to the team later today.</v>

00:03:11.908 --> 00:03:15.982
<v Marcus Lee>Who’s taking the action to update the policy doc in Confluence?</v>

00:03:16.858 --> 00:03:22.784
<v Samantha Ortiz>I can do it. I’ll draft the changes and share for review by end of day.</v>

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:26.823
<v Derek Patel>Thanks Sam! I’ll help with the examples section.</v>

00:03:27.567 --> 00:03:31.641
<v Rachel Evans>Perfect. Let’s aim to have the updated doc ready for Monday.</v>

00:03:33.382 --> 00:03:37.826
<v Jessica Tran>Switching gears—has anyone heard back from FleetSafe about our background check renewal?</v>

00:03:38.295 --> 00:03:47.184
<v Marcus Lee>Yeah, I got their quote yesterday. It’s a 7% increase over last year, but they’re adding real-time monitoring. I’ll forward the details after this.</v>

00:03:48.862 --> 00:03:52.936
<v Samantha Ortiz>Do we need to get legal to review the new terms?</v>

00:03:53.579 --> 00:03:57.283
<v Derek Patel>Probably a good idea. I’ll loop in Priya from legal.</v>

00:03:58.897 --> 00:04:01.490
<v Rachel Evans>Thanks, Derek. Anything else before we wrap?</v>

00:04:01.916 --> 00:04:03.416
<v Jessica Tran>Nothing from me.</v>

00:04:04.124 --> 00:04:05.624
<v Marcus Lee>All good here.</v>

00:04:07.283 --> 00:04:08.783
<v Samantha Ortiz>I’m set.</v>

00:04:09.355 --> 00:04:11.577
<v Derek Patel>Same. Have a good one, everyone!</v>

00:04:13.011 --> 00:04:14.511
<v Rachel Evans>Thanks all. Meeting adjourned.</v>
